# Course prerequisite rules — DS Pathway

This project models UNC Charlotte B.S. Data Science course prerequisites/corequisites
in `src/data/prerequisites.js` and enforces them in `src/lib/eligibility.js`. These
rules exist to prevent the app from ever recommending or allowing registration for
a course a student isn't actually eligible for.

## Source of truth

- `src/lib/eligibility.js` is the ONLY place eligibility is decided. Nothing else —
  not a prompt to an LLM, not inline logic in a component, not a one-off check in
  the registration flow — may independently decide whether a student can take a
  course. If new code needs an eligibility check, import and call
  `checkEligibility` / `getEligibleCourses` from that file. Do not reimplement the
  logic elsewhere.
- `src/data/prerequisites.js` is the ONLY place prerequisite/corequisite data lives.
  Every entry must carry a source comment: either a `catalog.charlotte.edu`
  `preview_course_nopop.php` URL (authoritative, per-course) or another named
  official UNC Charlotte source if the catalog page couldn't be found.
- Courses with confirmed no course-completion prerequisites go in `VERIFIED_NO_PREREQ`
  (gen-ed / intro entries reviewed on purpose). Catalog courses not in
  `PREREQUISITES` or `VERIFIED_NO_PREREQ` are **unverified and ineligible** —
  never fall back to implicit "no prerequisite."
- Entries without a verified source must NOT be added as empty rules to make a
  course eligible. Leave them unlisted until catalog data is confirmed.

## When adding or editing a course's rule

1. Look up the real prerequisite/corequisite text at catalog.charlotte.edu
   (search "`<SUBJECT> <NUMBER>` UNC Charlotte catalog", then use the
   `preview_course_nopop.php?catoid=...&coid=...` page for that course).
2. Encode it using the existing `Rule` shape: `{ all: [...] }` for "all of these
   required" and `{ oneOf: [...] }` for "any one of these satisfies it".
3. Cite the exact URL in a comment next to the entry.
4. If a prerequisite is a placement level or test score rather than a course code
   (e.g. STAT 1222's MATH placement requirement), do NOT encode it as a `Rule` —
   leave a comment explaining it's a placement gate and surface it as a static UI
   note instead, since this engine only models course-completion prerequisites.
5. Don't silently drop a course's rule to "no prerequisite" just because you
   couldn't find one — mark it `TODO: verify` instead so it's visibly unverified.

## When Gemini/AI is involved in course recommendations

- Any AI call (advisor chat, course recommender, etc.) must only ever be given
  courses that already passed `getEligibleCourses(...)`. Never hand the model the
  full catalog and ask it to reason about eligibility itself.
- Treat the model's output as untrusted: after it returns recommended course
  codes, filter them again against the eligible set server-side before returning
  anything to the frontend. If a returned code isn't in the eligible set, drop it
  — don't pass it through.

## Tests

- `src/lib/eligibility.js` must keep its unit tests passing (or gain new ones) for
  any change to rule-checking logic: verified-no-prereq courses eligible,
  unverified courses blocked, missing prereqs blocking eligibility, `oneOf` rules
  satisfied by any single listed course, and coreqs satisfiable by either
  `completed` or `inProgress`.
- Any change to `prerequisites.js` that alters an existing course's rule should be
  called out explicitly in the PR/commit description, since it changes what
  students are told they can register for.
